  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nDry sub-700mb air has continued to ensure any lift resulting in\nprecipitation formation only translates to isolated, brief\nsprinkles for portions of eastern OH through northwest PA\ntoday/tonight.  \n\nStrengthening warm, moist advection in southwest flow plus\nheight rises aloft results in high temperatures approaching 20\ndegrees above the daily averages Tuesday/Wednesday. Though no\nrecords are forecasted to be broken, there are scenarios where\nenough cloud cover limiting nocturnal cooling could result in a\nfew instances of near to record high minimum temperatures.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nModels have fairly high consistency in lifting an initially\nclosed upper low and its surface low center NW of the region\nWednesday into Thursday morning, with the surface low becoming\noccluded near central MI. The result is broad ascent within WSW\nflow combining with weakening surface convergence along the cold\nfront to produce swaths of rain showers traversing the regional\nwith a low probability for lightning (less than 15%). Dry\nadvection plus dry slotting aloft quickly ends rain chances\npost-frontal Thursday while slowly dropping temperature (but\nremaining above normal) and waiting for the next trough axis to\ncross with marginal precipitation chances Friday/Saturday.\n\nMean ensemble model accumulations for Wednesday/Thursday range \nbetween 0.25\" to 1\" that preclude flooding concerns. Lower QPF \nscenarios exist where forcing splits between a northern/southern\nembedded jet that dampens lift to keep rain on the lighter \nside. Higher end QPF scenarios (which could touch 2\") combine a\nbetter positioned jet with a slimmer of buoyancy to better \nmaximize the 95th percentile PWAT values in the region for more \nefficient rains. Even these high- end scenarios is unlikely to \nresult in flooding; the most likely impact is nuisance flooding \naided by clogged storm drains from recent leaf loss.",
